# Metabase Embedding SDK rules

When writing or editing code that uses the Metabase Embedding SDK:

## Imports

- Always import from `@metabase/embedding-sdk-react`. Never import from internal Metabase paths.
- The SDK package version must match the Metabase instance major version. For example, if the instance is `v1.60.x`, use `@metabase/embedding-sdk-react@0.60.x`.

## Component structure

- Wrap all embedded components in a single `MetabaseProvider` at the top of the component tree (or in a shared layout/provider component). Do not nest multiple `MetabaseProvider` instances.
- Use `StaticDashboard` for read-only embeds and `InteractiveDashboard` for embeds where users can explore and filter.
- Pass numeric IDs (e.g. `dashboardId`) as integer literals directly in the component — e.g. `dashboardId={7}`. Dashboard IDs are not secrets; do not wrap them in env vars or helper functions. If the ID comes from a URL param (always a string), `parseInt` it before passing. Note: entity IDs are strings — do not `parseInt` all IDs indiscriminately.

## Authentication

**The default and required authentication method is JWT SSO.** API keys grant admin-level Metabase access and must never be exposed to end users, so they are not suitable for production embeds. JWT SSO is the fastest path from no embedding to a production-ready embed.

- Do not generate any code that references `apiKey`, `METABASE_API_KEY`, `api-key`, `x-api-key`, or any API key variant — not even as a fallback, development shortcut, or example. Only deviate if the user explicitly asks for API key auth and confirms they understand the security implications.
- Auth is handled via a server-side JWT signing endpoint. `MetabaseProvider` receives the URL of that endpoint, never a raw key.
- The auth config prop name and shape passed to `MetabaseProvider` changed in Metabase v57. Check the versioned `llms.txt` (`https://www.metabase.com/docs/v0.{MAJOR}/llms.txt`) to confirm the correct prop names before writing auth config code.
- The JWT auth endpoint must return a signed token in the shape described in the version-specific docs. Do not assume the response shape from training data.
- Never hardcode JWT secrets or tokens. Keep them server-side only — never in `VITE_`, `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, or any other browser-accessible env var.

## Environment variables

- Prefix browser-visible values (instance URL, auth endpoint URL) with the framework's public env convention (e.g. `NEXT_PUBLIC_METABASE_URL` for Next.js, `VITE_METABASE_URL` for Vite).
- JWT secrets and signing logic must live server-side only. Never expose them to the browser.

## TypeScript

- Use the types exported by `@metabase/embedding-sdk-react` (e.g. `MetabaseAuthConfig`) rather than writing ad-hoc inline types for config objects.
